==English==

===Etymology===
See {{m|en|crack}}.

The origin of the slang sense “skilled (at a game)” is disputed, but may derive from the much older term {{m|en|crack shot||one who is skilled at shooting}}. {{etystub|en}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kɹækt/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-cracked.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ækt|s=1}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[broken|Broken]] so that [[crack]]s appear on, or under, the [[surface]].
#: {{syn|en|crazed}}
# Broken into coarse pieces.
# {{lb|en|of a voice}} [[harsh|Harsh]] or [[dissonant]].
#: {{syn|en|discordant|dissonant|harsh|inharmonious|raspy|rough}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} Extremely good at something (usually a [[video game]]).
#: {{ux|en|20 kills? Dude, you're '''cracked'''.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} [[crazy|Crazy]]; [[crackpot]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:insane}}

